;;; `std.algebra` — the seven **checks**, in the direction that does not
;;; refute: `irreflexive`, `antisymmetric`, `asymmetric`, `connex`, `empty`,
;;; `functional`, `injective`.
;;;
;;; ── Why this file exists before the seven that follow it ───────────────────
;;;
;;; A refutation rule has two tests and only one of them finds a bug. "It fires
;;; and the answer is ⊥" is checked by `09_…_violated.ein` and its six
;;; siblings, one rule each. This file is the other one: **seven checks
;;; loaded, activated, and satisfied**, so that a guard which admitted too much
;;; would turn a program with a perfectly ordinary model into a contradiction.
;;;
;;; That is the shape of the bug this whole phase was opened by — a
;;; `(neq …)` guard that was wrong for a year and that every *positive* test
;;; passed. The census's §7 counts 27 `neq` guards over 25 rules and says the
;;; identical case is what each of them is for; three of the seven below are
;;; exactly that shape.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to them ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   lt        a strict total order on three nodes — A<B<C, all three edges
;;;             authored. It satisfies four checks at once, and each of them
;;;             fails on a *different* single added edge:
;;;               irreflexive     (lt A A)
;;;               asymmetric      (lt B A)   — any mutual pair, self-loops too
;;;               antisymmetric   (lt B A)   — a mutual pair between distinct
;;;                                            endpoints, so the `neq` guard is
;;;                                            what separates it from asymmetric
;;;               connex          drop (lt A C), leaving A and C incomparable
;;;   pick      a partial injection {A↦X, B↦Y}: one image each, one source each
;;;               functional      (pick A Y)
;;;               injective       (pick B X)
;;;   loopy     one self-loop, tagged `antisymmetric` and nothing else
;;;   nothing   declared, tagged `(empty nothing)`, and authored with no edge
;;;
;;; `loopy` is the `neq` guard's own test, and the reason it is a fourth
;;; relation rather than a fifth edge on `lt`. `antisymmetric` means
;;; R(a,b) ∧ R(b,a) ⟹ a = b, so a self-loop is *legal* and the rule's
;;; `(neq ?a ?b)` is what makes it legal: the match finds `(loopy A A)` twice,
;;; and only the guard stops it concluding ⊥. Drop that guard and this file
;;; refutes — which is the failure mode `08_checks_satisfied` exists to have.
;;; The same shape covers three more guards for free: `connex`'s `(neq ?a ?b)`
;;; against the pair (A,A), and `functional`'s and `injective`'s
;;; `(neq ?b ?c)` against each `pick` edge matched with itself.
;;;
;;; `nothing` is the one relation here the expectation cannot mention: naming a
;;; relation closes it, and there is no fact of it to name — Q-M1c.6, reached
;;; from the corpus. What stands in for the missing clause is the verdict: had
;;; `empty` fired, this file would be `(false)` and no `(model …)` would match.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The whole claim is "a model exists, and it is the authored one". Every rule
;;; here asserts `(false)`, so *any* of the seven firing replaces the model
;;; with a contradiction, and closing `lt` and `pick` says the seven did not
;;; quietly add an edge either.

(import std.algebra
  :symbols (irreflexive antisymmetric asymmetric connex empty
            functional injective))

(relation instance Thing Type)
(relation lt       Node Node :why "{?1} comes before {?2}")
(relation pick     Node Mark :why "{?1} is marked {?2}")
(relation loopy    Node Node)
(relation nothing  Node Node)

(instance A Node) (instance B Node) (instance C Node)

(irreflexive   lt)
(antisymmetric lt)
(asymmetric    lt)
(connex        lt instance Node)
(functional    pick)
(injective     pick)
(antisymmetric loopy)          ; a self-loop is legal, and `neq` is why
(empty         nothing)

(lt A B) (lt B C) (lt A C)
(pick A X) (pick B Y)
(loopy A A)

(query
  :goal (lt ?x ?y)
  :no-hypothesis (instance lt pick loopy nothing)
  :expect (model (lt A B) (lt B C) (lt A C)
                 (pick A X) (pick B Y)
                 (loopy A A)))
